               <msContents>
                  <msItem class="#liturgica" n="1" xml:id="MS_Liturg_104-item1"><locus from="1r" to="123v" type="inferred"/>
                     <title key="work_10576" type="desc">Book of Hours, Use of Sarum</title>
                     <msItem >
                        <note>Office of the Virgin with the Hours of the Cross (fol. 48: Patris sapientia). The Hours of the Cross incorporate a unique Middle English verse text, <ref target="https://dimev.net/record.php?recID=5069">DIMEV 5069 / NIMEV 3230</ref> (fol. 49: Swete ihesu cryst goddis sone).</note>
                     </msItem>
                     
                     <msItem>
                        <note>Penitential Psalms (fol. 89) with litany (fol. 108v). </note></msItem><msItem><note>Late 14th-century and 15th-century additions: prayers and commemorations including St Christopher and St Anne (fol. iv-v), psalms (fol. 119), Salve regina mater misericordie (fol. 122), order of the Gradual Psalms (fol. 123).</note>
                     </msItem>
                     <textLang mainLang="la" otherLangs="emn">Latin and Middle English </textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem>
                     <note>Added, 15th century, on fol. 123v: (1) a charm for jaundice in French and Latin (2) two charms in Latin with English rubrics, (i) 'þis ys good for bytyng of a ...d dog' (ii) 'for to staunc blod'. </note>
                     <textLang mainLang="la" otherLangs="xno enm">Latin, Anglo-Norman, Middle English</textLang>
                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
